# hilavitkutin-api: Design

Consumer-facing types, traits, and platform contracts. Downstream
consumer applications depend on this crate to define WorkUnits. The
engine crate (hilavitkutin) also depends on it. `#![no_std]`, no
alloc. All index, count, and size types use arvo newtypes.

Depends on `hilavitkutin-ctx` for its `provider_generic!` /
`provider_generic2!` macros and the `Context<P>` provider-tuple
wrapper. WU execute parameter types and the WU-context accessor
traits are expressed via those macros; see "WU Context providers"
below.

The crate uses `provider` as a single workspace-wide vocabulary.
Two trait families share the noun:

1. The `Provider` trait declared in this section gates registration
   on the SchedulerBuilder. Every value passed to `.with(value)`
   impls it. See `## Provider, the unified registration contract`.
2. The ctx-framework accessor traits (`HasResourceProvider`,
   `HasColumnReader`, etc.) generated by `provider_generic!`
   expose runtime providers on the WU Context tuple. See
   `## WU Context providers` below.

The two families connect: a `Resource<T>` registered via
`.with(Resource::new(value))` becomes reachable in WU Context
tuples through `HasResourceProvider<R>`. Registration is the
build-time half; accessors are the run-time half. Both refer to
the same concept (a thing the scheduler provides), so both wear
the `provider` label.

## Provider, the unified registration contract

`SchedulerBuilder` takes one verb: `.with(value)`. Every type
accepted by `.with` impls the sealed `Provider` trait. The trait
gates registration and routes per-kind typestate updates through
its `Dispatch` associated type.

```rust
pub trait Provider {
    type Init;
    const KIND: ProviderKind;
    type Dispatch: Dispatch;
}
```

The associated `Init` names the construction-time value type. For a
store that holds values it is the value's type: the inner `T` of a
`Resource<T>`, and the element type of a `Column<T>` or a
`Buffered<T, M>`, whose start it is. For the zero-data wrappers
(`Accum<T>`, `Virtual<T>`, `LinkedBin<dyn Trait>`) it is `()`. For
unit-struct WorkUnits, Kits, and platform-impl types it is `Self` (the
registration value IS the construction value).

`Resource::new(value)` and a column's `start()` and `start_from(value)`
return a value-carrying carrier rather than dropping the value. The
builder moves each registered input onto a staged value list at `.with`,
so a registered store value is retained until `build()` moves it into
the `MemoryProvider`-allocated arena. The zero-data wrappers
(`Accum<T>`, `Virtual<T>`) carry no value; their `Init` is `()`.

`Dispatch` is the per-kind typestate router (next subsection). The
constant `KIND: ProviderKind` is a documentation and debugging aid;
load-bearing dispatch is via `Provider::Dispatch`, not the constant.

The trait carries `#[diagnostic::on_unimplemented]`. The message
points at the constructors a consumer reaches for: `Resource::new(value)`,
`Column::<T>::start()`, `Virtual::<T>::new()`, `LinkedBin::<dyn TraitFamily>::new()`,
plus the unit-struct WorkUnit, Kit, and platform-impl shapes. A type
that does not impl `Provider` cannot reach `.with`; the diagnostic
names the gap directly.

`Provider` is sealed via a private `Token` pattern. Consumer crates
cannot impl `Provider` directly; the substrate mints the trait for
the four router shapes (Unit, Store, Kit, Platform) plus the
consumer-reachable unit-struct shapes (WorkUnits, Kits, platform
impls).

## Dispatch: per-kind typestate routing

```rust
pub trait Dispatch {
    type NextWus<Wus>;
    type NextStores<Stores>;
    type NextPlatform<Platform>;
}
```

Each generic-associated type computes the next typestate accumulator
when a provider of the corresponding kind registers. The four router
structs sit beside `Provider`:

| Router | Routes |
|---|---|
| `UnitDispatch<W>` | Prepends `W` to the `Wus` accumulator. |
| `StoreDispatch<S>` | Prepends `S` to the `Stores` accumulator. |
| `KitDispatch<K>` | Concats `K::Units` into `Wus` and `K::Owned` into `Stores`. |
| `PlatformDispatch<P>` | Prepends `P` to the platform-tuple accumulator. |

For routers that do not affect a given accumulator, the
corresponding GAT is identity. `UnitDispatch<W>::NextWus<Wus> =
Cons<W, Wus>`; its `NextStores` and `NextPlatform` pass `Stores` and
`Platform` through unchanged. `StoreDispatch<S>::NextStores<Stores>
= Cons<S, Stores>`; its other two GATs are identity.
`KitDispatch<K>::NextWus<Wus> = <K::Units as Concat<Wus>>::Out`;
analogous for stores; platform GAT is identity.
`PlatformDispatch<P>::NextPlatform<Platform> = Cons<P, Platform>`;
its other two GATs are identity.

A single blanket impl on `SchedulerBuilder` over `P: Provider` reads
`P::Dispatch` and walks the per-kind GAT. The trait solver fails on
`Provider` directly when a non-`Provider` value is passed to
`.with`, surfacing the right diagnostic at the call site. Per-kind
blankets would route the failure through the wrong trait first (an
error pointing at `WorkUnit` when the user passed a `u32`).
Validated empirically; see
`mock/research/sketches/202605091700-builder-provider-shape/`.

## ProviderKind

```rust
pub enum ProviderKind {
    WorkUnit,
    Resource,
    Column,
    Virtual,
    LinkedBin,
    Kit,
    Memory,
    Threads,
    Clock,
}
```

Documentation and debugging aid only. The const projection
`Provider::KIND` lets diagnostics print "you passed a Column
provider where the bound expected a Memory provider"; it does not
participate in dispatch.

## Store types (domain 06)

Five store markers and one scheduling mechanism. By what it holds, state
is still one of three things: a collection of records, a single value,
or a signal with no data. The buffered column and the accumulator are
collections, and each has a marker of its own beside the column. All of
them are views over one `ColumnStorage` (see "Unified columnar storage"
below); they differ in intent and access, not in backing.

```rust
pub struct Resource<T>(PhantomData<T>); // singleton, one value
pub struct Column<T>(PhantomData<T>);   // collection, morsel-chunked
pub struct Buffered<T, M = Snapshot>(PhantomData<(T, M)>); // a column in two sides, read frozen and written live
pub struct Accum<T>(PhantomData<T>);    // a column written by append, grown from zero each frame
pub struct Virtual<T>(PhantomData<T>);  // zero-data, DAG edge only

pub struct Snapshot;                         // the live side is copied into the frozen side
pub struct Exchange;                         // the two sides trade places
pub trait BufferMode: Sealed + 'static {}    // the two above and no other
```

`Buffered<T, M>` is a column held in two sides, each of the record
count. Every read of it sees the frozen side, which is the store as it
stood when the pass began, and every write lands on the live side. After
the last unit of a pass the live side of every buffered store the pass
changed becomes its frozen side, and a store the pass did not change is
left as it is and costs nothing. `M` is how the one side becomes the
other, chosen where the store is named and settled at compile time.
Under `Snapshot` the live side always holds the whole store, a unit may
write any part of it, and the boundary copies the live side into the
frozen one. Under `Exchange` the two sides trade places with no copy.
The side a unit then writes into holds the values of two passes before,
so the store is right only where a unit that writes it writes every
record, which a value function does. `Snapshot` is the default since it
is right under any writer. "Reading a store as it stood when the pass
began" below has the rules.

A column, plain or buffered, is registered with the value every record
of it starts from, and its element type decides which ways of naming
that value there are. Every record of a plain column holds it before the
first pass, and so does every record of both sides of a buffered one, so
a read of the first pass sees it wherever no unit has written yet. No
read of a column reaches a record nothing wrote.

```rust
pub trait Starts: Sealed { type Value; }                     // `Column<T>` and `Buffered<T, M>`, and no other
pub struct Started<S: Starts>(/* the start of the store `S` */);

impl<T> Column<T> {
    pub const fn new() -> Self;                              // the marker a unit names the store by
    pub const fn start_from(value: T) -> Started<Self>;      // any element type
}
impl<T: HasTrivialCtor> Column<T> {
    pub const fn start() -> Started<Self>;                   // the type's trivial constructor
}
// `Buffered<T, M>` has the same three.

pub trait StartsFromDefault: Starts {
    fn start() -> Started<Self>;                             // the type's default
}
impl<S: Starts> StartsFromDefault for S where S::Value: Default {}
```

`Starts` is what the two columns share: a store that holds a value of
its element type at every record. `Started<S>` is the one carrier of a
start, for either. The three functions are written on each marker and
not once over the trait, since the first tier has to be an inherent
function (below) and an inherent function is written on a type.

`start()` reads the element type's own start in a fixed sequence: its
trivial constructor (`HasTrivialCtor`, from notko) where it has one, else
its `Default`. For a type that has both it is the constructor. For a type
that has neither there is no `start()`, the call does not compile, and
`start_from(value)` is the one way to register the column. `start_from`
is there for every element type, so a type with a start of its own can
still be started from another value. The marker alone is not registered:
`.with(Column::<T>::new())` and `.with(Buffered::<T>::new())` do not
compile, since the marker says nothing of what the first pass reads. The
error says so: the note on `BuilderInput` names `start()` and
`start_from(value)` as the two ways a column is registered.

The constructor is ahead of the default for what each is. A type that
implements `HasTrivialCtor` beside `Default` has said what it is made as
from nothing, and the constructor is callable in a constant, so a column
of such a type can be registered from one. The default of a type that
has both is still within reach, by the trait's own path or as
`start_from(T::default())`.

The sequence is not two blanket impls of one trait, which would overlap
for a type that has both. The first tier is an inherent function under
its bound and the second a trait's function under its own, and an
inherent function is found first and passed over where its bound does
not hold. So `StartsFromDefault` has to be in scope where the default
tier is called, and the choice is made where `start()` is written, from
what is known of the type there: a generic caller that knows only
`T: Default` gets the default.
<!-- FIXME(placeholders_tagged_df2e4da2d376): a tier ahead of the two, for a type whose all-zeros pattern is a value of it, waits on a trait notko does not have -->

The start is one value for the whole store. Data that differs from
record to record is written by a unit in a pass, as any column is
written. A column a kit owns is named by its marker in the kit's list
and has no start there.
<!-- FIXME(placeholders_tagged_ac91dfb44d6e): a kit names the columns it owns by marker alone, and what such a column starts from is undecided -->

`Accum<T>` is a marker of its own on the same pattern. It reserves a
capacity at build, is reset to no values at the top of a frame and grows
as units append during it. The values appended in a frame are what the
close of that frame reads (see "Maintenance units and the close" below).

The buffered column and the accumulator are markers, and not a column
under a wrapper in a unit's list, for what each form does in the types
and in the plumbing. A column is one run of records that lasts across
frames and is written by position. An accumulator is a region per core
with a length of its own, empty at the top of every frame and written at
its end. A buffered column is two runs of records, one read and one
written. With a marker, the store that is registered says which of the
three storages it gets, and `StoreDispatch` reserves it with no look at
any unit. A positional write to an accumulator or to a buffered column,
or an append to a column, fails at the accessor's own bound in the unit
that tried it. A column of `T`, a buffered column of `T` and an
accumulator of `T` are three stores. With a wrapper over `Column<T>`,
the storage a store gets would follow from how every unit lists it, a
unit appending to a store another unit writes by position could only be
refused when the whole schedule is built, and the stores of `T` would be
one name.

The scheduler sees StoreId pairs for edge construction. Does not care
about cardinality. `Write<Resource<X>> → Read<Resource<X>>` builds
the same edge as `Write<Column<Y>> → Read<Column<Y>>`.

`StoreId` is a dense index assigned at plan time. Wraps an arvo
fixed-point newtype (not raw u16). `AccessMask` is the arvo-bitmask
`Mask64` (<=64 stores) or `Mask256` (<=256 stores). All scheduler ops
= single-instruction bitwise.

`AccessSet` is the api's bound over the type-level list of
`notko-hlist`. `Empty` and `Cons<H, T>` are that crate's types,
re-exported from this crate's root so that a unit's crate names one
crate, and consumer code meets them either directly or through the
`read!` / `write!` macros (which expand to nested `Cons` cells). The
api declares no list of its own: `Contains`, `ContainsAll` and `Concat`
are the shared crate's as well, and `AccessSet` adds the count as a
`USize` and the bound that says a list is made of members: store
markers, and the wrappers over a store marker described under
"Members of an access set" below.

The lists are compile-time store descriptors. Plan phase converts to runtime AccessMask via
monomorphised generic functions (the monomorphised function IS the
type identity, no TypeId, no std::any).

ZST erasure: column marker types constrain the DAG, enforce type
safety, and determine morsel sizes at compile time. Erased to plain
indices by execution time.

### Store wrappers as Providers

A store reaches `.with` on the SchedulerBuilder through a `Provider`
whose `Dispatch` is `StoreDispatch` of the store's marker. A store that
holds values is registered with a carrier of its value, and a store that
holds none with its marker:

```rust
.with(Resource::new(value))            // the resource's value
.with(Column::<T>::start())            // the start of every record, from the element type
.with(Column::<T>::start_from(value))  // the start of every record, named
.with(Buffered::<T>::start())          // the same two for a buffered column,
.with(Buffered::<T, Exchange>::start_from(value)) // in either mode
.with(Accum::<T>::new())               // no values until a unit appends
.with(Virtual::<T>::new())             // no data
```

`Resource::new(value)` returns a `StagedResource<T>`, with `Init = T`
and `Dispatch = StoreDispatch<Resource<T>>`. A column's `start()` and
`start_from(value)` return a `Started<S>` for its marker `S`, with
`Init` the element type and `Dispatch = StoreDispatch<S>`. In both the
store list holds the marker and a unit names the store by it. "Store
types" above has which of the two calls exists for which element type.
`Accum<T>` and `Virtual<T>` are their own providers, with `Init = ()`
and `Dispatch = StoreDispatch<Self>`.

`Column<T>` and `Buffered<T, M>` are not providers: the marker of a
store that holds a value at every record says nothing of that value.

`Column`, `Buffered`, `Accum` and `Virtual` impl `HasTrivialCtor` (from
notko), the trait's const-callable zero-arg constructor of the marker.
`Resource` has no marker constructor of its own and does not impl it.

## Record counts

A scheduler holds one record count, the one `build` takes, and every
column it holds has that many records. `Column<T>` carries no count and
no second parameter. An accumulator's capacity is that same count.
Resources and virtuals hold no record count. A consumer whose record
sets differ in count builds a scheduler for each set.
